<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="2" to="4" step="1"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="b" from="3" to="5" step="1" exclude="$a"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="c" from="2" to="5" step="1" exclude="$a $b"/>

  <selectFromSequence name="p" from="3" to="11" step="4"/>
  <selectFromSequence name="q" from="17" to="19" step="2"/>


</setup>
<p>
  Each of the following infinite series of real numbers corresponds to one of the five known Taylor series centered at <m>a = 0</m> of important functions that we have developed to date.  By recognizing the pattern in the coefficients, use the known Taylor series to find the exact value of the infinite sum.
</p>

<p>
  <ol marker="a.">
    <li>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>\displaystyle 1 - $a + \frac{$a^2}{2!} - \frac{$a^3}{3!} + \frac{$a^4}{4!} - \cdots =</m></label>e^(-$a)</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>\displaystyle \frac{1}{$b} - \frac{1}{2}\left( \frac{1}{$b} \right)^2 + \frac{1}{3}\left( \frac{1}{$b} \right)^3 - \frac{1}{4}\left( \frac{1}{$b} \right)^4 +  \cdots=</m></label>ln(1+1/$b)</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>\displaystyle $c - \frac{$c^3}{3!} + \frac{$c^5}{5!} - \frac{$c^7}{7!} + \cdots =</m></label>sin($c)</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
    <li>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>\displaystyle 1 + \frac{$p}{$q} + \left(\frac{$p}{$q} \right)^2 + \left(\frac{$p}{$q} \right)^3 + \left(\frac{$p}{$q} \right)^4 + \cdots =</m></label>1/(1-($p/$q))</answer>
      </p>
    </li>
  </ol>
</p>